            <ArticleTitle>The effect of horticultural therapy on the quality of life of palliative care patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>278-291</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/07347332.2017.1286699</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Palliative care patients experience a variety of needs and perceive their quality of life as being only fair. This study adopted a single-group repeated-measure design to investigate the effect of horticultural therapy on the quality of life of palliative care patients using the Quality of Life Concern in End of Life Questionnaire. Significant differences in the domains of &quot;existential distress&quot; and &quot;health care concern&quot; were observed immediately postintervention and at 4 weeks postintervention, respectively. No other significant differences were seen in the other domains or in the total mean score of the outcome measure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
